        <content>You are given a JSON array of product/material rows extracted from a construction 
specification document, pulled independently from multiple document chunks. Rows 
may be duplicated or fragmented — for example, the same set of interchangeable 
products may appear as separate rows each listing the others as &quot;approvedAlternates.&quot;

Consolidate this into a clean JSON array following these rules:

1. Merge rows that describe the same interchangeable product group (same 
  application, overlapping alternates) into a single row. Pick one primary 
  productName/manufacturer (prefer the one explicitly marked as &quot;basis-of-design&quot; 
  in context, or the first-listed if unclear) and list the others in 
  approvedAlternates.
2. Remove exact duplicate rows (identical specSection + productName).
3. Do not merge rows that have different applications, even if from the same 
  manufacturer.
4. Preserve all fields: specSection, application, productName, manufacturer, 
  approvedAlternates, substitutionAllowed.
5. If substitutionAllowed conflicts across merged rows, keep &quot;N&quot; if any instance 
  says N (most restrictive wins).
6. Sort the output array by specSection ascending.

Output ONLY the resulting JSON array. No markdown code fences, no commentary, 
no explanation.

Input data:
{!$Input:Product_Table_Json}
</content>
